<!-- Figure: an illustration of a designer prompting an agent, and a prototype taking shape. -->
<ProseBlock
  heading="Prototyping with AI"
  image={placeholder}
  imageAlt="Placeholder: an illustration of a designer prompting an agent, and a prototype taking shape"
  imageCaption="Designers began prompting prototypes into being."
>
  <p>
    At the same time, Google was pushing hard to adopt its own AI code editor,
    known publicly as Antigravity. Designers were starting to use Gemini and
    Antigravity to build working prototypes instead of static mockups. The
    promise was higher fidelity, faster. In practice, it was still messy.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="My role">
  <p>
    I was a UX Designer II on contract with the merchant team. I designed and
    built the kit myself, mostly during our 8-week Business Home project and
    alongside my design work on it. That’s how I like to work: I keep improving
    my process, picking up new tools and skills as I go, without slowing down
    delivery.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our Design Manager and another Lead UX Designer owned the team’s shared code
    sandbox, a separate effort to centralize prototyping and sync it with
    production code. I shared my findings with them, and piloted my kit with the
    designers I worked with directly.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<!--
  Figure: a diagram contrasting one monolithic file (styles, views and logic
  tangled together) with a structured project (tokens, components and views in
  separate files).
-->
<ProseBlock
  heading="The first sandbox"
  image={placeholder}
  imageAlt="Placeholder: a diagram contrasting one monolithic file with a structured project of tokens, components, and views in separate files"
  imageCaption="One file holding everything meant every change touched everything."
>
  <p>
    Our Design Manager and another Lead UX Designer set out to build a shared
    code sandbox where designers could prototype with AI. When my teammates and
    I tried the first version, we kept running into the same problems: results
    that didn’t match from one turn to the next, and long turns that burned
    through tokens.
  </p>
  <p>
    When I dug into it, the cause looked structural. The sandbox produced a
    single HTML file, with every style and every view packed into its header.
    Each request made the agent read and rewrite the whole thing. That likely
    bloated its context, and it meant a small hallucination anywhere could
    ripple through the whole prototype.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<!--
  Figure: an illustration of the kit's folder structure (tokens, components,
  views, skills), drawn from the independent version.
-->
<ProseBlock
  heading="A different path"
  image={placeholder}
  imageAlt="Placeholder: the kit’s folder structure of tokens, components, views, and skills, drawn from the independent version"
  imageCaption="Separating concerns gave the agent less to read and less to break."
>
  <p>
    Over the summer the sandbox team kept iterating, focused on centralizing the
    sandbox and syncing it with production code. Those were the right goals for
    them, but as a contractor I couldn’t access the monorepo where production
    code lived. So I decided to build my own kit.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was building it mainly for myself, but I wanted it sturdy enough for other
    designers to pick up. Years of working with startups and frontend teams, and
    building my own website, had given me a working sense of how good codebases
    are organized. I wanted to bring that structure to AI prototyping. When I
    started, I shared my diagnosis with the sandbox team as my reasoning, and
    they later applied some of those tips in their own work.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<ProseBlock heading="Starting with a conversation">
  <p>
    I started the way I’d start with an engineering partner: by talking it
    through. I laid out what prototyping needs, above all speed and flexibility.
    I listed the interactions that would need dynamic data or services, and
    asked Gemini to recommend frameworks and an architecture. It suggested Vite
    for its speed and flexibility.
  </p>
  <DraftNote>
    <p>React on Vite? Anything Gemini recommended that you turned down?</p>
  </DraftNote>
</ProseBlock>

<!-- Figure: a layered diagram: tokens → components → agent rules → automated checks → prototype. -->
<ProseBlock
  heading="Guardrails for fidelity"
  layout="stacked"
  image={placeholder}
  imageAlt="Placeholder: a layered diagram of tokens, components, agent rules, and automated checks leading to the prototype"
  imageCaption="Each layer narrowed what the agent could get wrong."
>
  <p>
    A fast setup wasn’t enough. The prototypes also had to look and behave like
    our product, every time, so I added layers of guardrails. Design tokens for
    color, type and spacing meant styles came from one source. A central
    component library meant agents assembled screens from approved parts instead
    of inventing new ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    On top of that, agent instructions ruled out third-party libraries and raw
    values, and automated design checks confirmed that only approved tokens were
    used and no outside libraries crept in. Agents drift and hallucinate.
    Limiting them to approved tokens kept that drift from reaching the
    prototype.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

<!--
  Figure: a sync diagram: Figma variables and components on one side; JSON
  tokens, Code Connect and Storybook on the other; agent skills in between.
-->
<ProseBlock
  heading="One library, many teams"
  layout="stacked"
  image={placeholder}
  imageAlt="Placeholder: a sync diagram with Figma variables and components on one side, JSON tokens, Code Connect, and Storybook on the other, and agent skills in between"
  imageCaption="When the library changed in Figma, the kit could follow."
>
  <p>
    We were in the middle of a major release, with many teams working on
    different features and flows at once. In parallel, a team of visual
    designers was building our own fork of Material Design 3. The prototypes
    coming in from other designers were fragmented. Left alone, Gemini defaulted
    to plain Material Design, so prototypes didn’t match the new product’s
    direction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tokens alone weren’t enough. The kit had to stay in sync with Figma
    libraries that the visual designers kept changing. So I built a workflow to
    keep the two aligned. Figma’s design variables were exported as JSON tokens
    the kit could use directly. Agent skills, written from my own trial and
    error, told agents to inspect a frame’s layer hierarchy and match its
    structure in code. Figma Code Connect mapped design components to their
    coded versions, and Storybook let anyone browse and check every coded
    component.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

